^Furniture and home^

In this skill you will learn how to describe your home

A few weird remarks:

*saa* (he/she/it is allowed to)
*saa mennä* (one is allowed to go, you are allowed to go)
*pitää* (it is necessary to)
*pitää pestä* (one has to wash, it needs to be washed, there is a need to wash)
sen näkee (it can be seen, it's visible)

These sentences seem weird in English, and during this course you will encounter some sentences that seem to be bad English translations, but they only look 'a little off' because there is no real counterpart for some Finnish words.

For example, in English you say 'it is raining outside', but what is 'it' really? What rains?

The Finnish translation is 'sataa' (it is raining), and it is in the form where you would use 'hän' (he/she) or 'se' (it) but if you did, it would mean that a certain thing is literally raining, which is impossible. Nothing can rain, it just rains.

In the same vein, the word *saa* can be used together with *hän* to make *hän saa* (he gets, he is allowed to) but when used without the pronoun, *saa* just means (it is allowed), e.g. 'saa lukea' (it is allowed to read, or more correctly, reading is allowed). This is why you always have to include *hän, he* with a verb, otherwise the verb is abstract.

*hän saa lukea* - he is allowed to read
*saa lukea* - reading is allowed

But, as an answer to a question, instead of answering 'yes', the verb without the third person pronoun can be said back to the person who asks. (We will dive into this a little later in the course)

*- Lukeeko hän?* (Is he reading?) 
*- Lukee.* (Yes, he is reading)